ical-model-and-machine-learning">Difference between Statistical Model and Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-ranking-algorithms-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Ranking Algorithms in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/multicollinearity-causes-effects-and-detection">Multicollinearity: Causes, Effects and Detection</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-n-grams-model">Bag of N-Grams Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tf-idf-model">TF-IDF Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-probabilistic-training-data-sampling-methods-in-machine-learning">5 Probabilistic Training Data Sampling Methods in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-websites-to-download-pre-trained-machine-learning-models">5 Websites to Download Pre-trained Machine Learning Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/a-comprehensive-comparison-of-ml-experiment-tracking-tools">A Comprehensive Comparison of ML Experiment Tracking Tools</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/adam-optimizer">Adam Optimizer</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solution-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solution in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solutions-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solutions in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/arithmetic-geometric-and-harmonic-mean">Arithmetic Geometric and Harmonic Mean</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/assumptions-of-logistic-regression">Assumptions of Logistic Regression</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/attention-mechanism-in-machine-learning">Attention Mechanism in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/autoencoder-classification-in-keras">Autoencoder Classification in Keras</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/automating-api-testing-with-postman">Automating API Testing With Postman</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-words-bow-model-in-machine-learning">Bag of Words (BoW) Model in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/batch-normalization">Batch Normalization</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-books-for-natural-language-processing-with-python">Best Books for Natural Language Processing With Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-computer-vision-books">Best Computer Vision Books</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bleu-score-in-machine-learning">Bleu Score in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/building-a-machine-learning-regression-model-from-a-multivariate-nonlinear-dataset">Building a Machine Learning Regression Model from a Multivariate Nonlinear Dataset</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/complex-multi-panel-figures">Complex Multi Panel Figures</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conformal-prediction-for-machine-learning-classification">Conformal Prediction for Machine Learning Classification</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-types-in-statistics-used-for-machine-learning">Data Types in Statistics Used for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/demographic-based-recommender-systems">Demographic-Based Recommender Systems</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/differences-between-machine-learning-engineers-and-researchers">Differences Between Machine Learning Engineers And Researchers</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/distance-measures-for-machine-learning">Distance Measures for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/early-stopping-in-machine-learning">Early Stopping in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/f2-score">F2 Score</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/gradient-boosting-algorithm">Gradient Boosting Algorithm</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-do-you-increase-statistical-power">How do you increase Statistical power</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-softmax-activation-function-works">How Softmax Activation Function Works</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-calculate-correlation-between-variables-in-python">How to Calculate Correlation Between Variables in Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-causality-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Causality in Machine Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading4"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse4" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse4" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading4" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse4"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-science">Data Science Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/nlp">NLP Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading5"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse5" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse5" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading5" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse5"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/machine-learning-interview-questions">Top 50 Machine learning Interview Questions and Answers (2025)</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="bayes-theorem-in-machine-learning">next →</a> <a class="next" href="epoch">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">A Comparison of DNN, CNN, and LSTM Using TensorFlow/Keras</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>28 Feb 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 16 min read</span></div><h2 class="h2">Introduction</h2><p>In the field of Machine learning and Deep learning, there are various types of Neural network architecture that are commonly used to solve different kinds of problems. From these, DNNs, CNNs, and LSTMs are quite noticeable as they are designed with specific possibilities and purposes. Fortunately, TensorFlow / Keras, one of the successful deep learning frameworks, offers quite effective tools for the implementation of these architectures.</p><h2 class="h2">1. Deep Neural Networks (DNNs)</h2><h3 class="h3">Architecture</h3><p><strong>The deep neural network (DNN) is an ANN model containing one or more hidden layers between the input layer and the output layer, hence known as the latent space layer.</strong> It's made of a set of neurons, and each neuron in one layer is connected to every neuron in the next layer, thus creating a very dense network. The primary components of a DNN include:</p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Input Layer:</strong> Inputs the raw data set as the sample from which to work.</li><li><strong>Hidden Layers:</strong> Multilayers in which every neuron comes with having used a non-linear activation function on the summation of weighted inputs.</li><li><strong>Output Layer:</strong> This is responsible for the final output on the production line.</li></ul><p>Examples of activation functions used with <strong>DNNs are ReLU, Sigmoid and Tanh.</strong> These functions make the model non-linear, effectively enabling the model to capture non-linear patterns.</p><h3 class="h3">Strengths</h3><ol class="points"><li><strong>Versatility:</strong> The general ANN is called a DNN, and it can be used in different tasks, including classification and regression.</li><li><strong>Deep Representations:</strong> If made deeper enough, DNNs are capable of modeling complex data representations into a hierarchical structure.</li><li><strong>End-to-End Learning:</strong> One advantage of DNNs is that they can learn from scratch, which means very little preprocessing or feature extraction is required.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Weaknesses</h3><ol class="points"><li><strong>Overfitting:</strong> They also observed that adding a greater number of layers or neurons causes DNNs to overfit the training data and not generalize well to new data.</li><li><strong>Computationally Expensive:</strong> This is because training deep networks requires a certain level of computational intensity and time consumption.</li><li><strong>Vanishing/Exploding Gradients:</strong> There are cases where deep networks can produce either very small gradients or gradients that are very large and an issue for learning.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Use Cases</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Image Classification:</strong> DNNs can take an image and put it into one of the delivered categories.</li><li><strong>Natural Language Processing:</strong> Applied in text categorization and opinion mining.</li><li><strong>Regression Tasks:</strong> Continuous data output based on some input functions.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">2. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)</h2><h3 class="h3">Architecture</h3><p>The convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a type of neural network that is designed <strong>to work on grid data, such as images.</strong> CNNs incorporate convolution layers that involve convolution to identify localized features and patterns from the data. Key components of a CNN include:</p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Convolutional Layers:</strong> Convolutional layers work on the variations of the input data and identify pertinent features like edges, textures and other shapes.</li><li><strong>Activation Functions:</strong> ReLU is usually applied to introduce non-linearity into the model and, hence, improve a network's ability to learn complex data representations.</li><li><strong>Pooling Layers:</strong> One can downsample the feature maps, keeping important features' size notwithstanding but reducing the spatial extent. Some commonly used pooling methods are Max pooling and Average pooling.</li><li><strong>Fully Connected Layers:</strong> The high-level features extracted are then passed to the fully connected layers to aid in classification or regression.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Strengths</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Feature Extraction:</strong> CNNs can learn and co-extract hierarchical features from the image, so there is no need for people to manually build features.</li><li><strong>Spatial Hierarchies:</strong> They are good at modeling spatial hierarchies and local relations in the images.</li><li><strong>Parameter Sharing:</strong> In convolutional layers, too, filters are convoluted across the image space and, hence, share the same weightage across the regions. Hence, there are fewer parameters than fully connected layers, which are computationally expensive.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Weaknesses</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Data Hungry:</strong> It is unfortunate that most CNNs still require a large volume of labeled data for training.</li><li><strong>Computational Resources:</strong> As pointed out before, training CNNs can be computationally costly, especially with deep architectures.</li><li><strong>Limited Temporal Dynamics:</strong> Thus, CNNs are not designed to specifically address the sequential or temporal data common in many real-world applications.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Use Cases</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Image and Video Processing:</strong> Object detection, facial recognition, and image segmentation.</li><li><strong>Medical Image Analysis:</strong> Interpreting X-rays, MRIs and other imaging results.</li><li><strong>Visual Scene Understanding:</strong> Scene categorization and object detection.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">3. Long Short-Term Memory Networks (LSTMs)</h2><h3 class="h3">Architecture</h3><p><strong>Recurrent neural networks (RNNs), are specialized for modeling data sequences are known as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks.</strong> Memory cells of LSTMs enable them to store information for longer periods; this eliminates what is known as the vanishing gradient from problems linked to traditional RNNs. Key components of an LSTM include:</p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Memory Cells:</strong> Allow records to be kept in the long term.</li><li><strong>Gates:</strong> Regulate the signals that enter and the signals that are to be sent to the memory cells. These include:</li><li><strong>Forget Gate:</strong> Controls which information should be deleted from the memory cell.</li><li><strong>Input Gate:</strong> To write, the operation modifies the memory cell with new information.</li><li><strong>Output Gate:</strong> Depending on the content of that memory cell, it decides the output.</li><li><strong>Hidden State:</strong> Stands for the vector of the LSTM at time step t.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Strengths</h3><ol class="points"><li><strong>Long-Term Dependencies:</strong> LSTMs can model long-term dependencies as well as long-term dependencies of the underlying sequential data.</li><li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> The gating mechanism assists in directing and regulating the inflow and itself eliminates problems such as vanishing gradients.</li><li><strong>Versatility:</strong> Appropriate sequence-based learning tasks that use multiple inputs and outputs and yet still can benefit from off-shelf models that help to simplify the workflow.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Weaknesses</h3><ol class="points"><li><strong>Complexity:</strong> LSTMs are generally more complicated than basic RNNs, which makes their training and tuning a bit challenging.</li><li><strong>Computational Cost:</strong> They are more computationally intensive than their counterparts partly because of their structural design and computations.</li><li><strong>Training Time:</strong> Training LSTMs is a slow affair, and it is even worse when dealing with long sequences.</li></ol><h3 class="h3">Use Cases</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Natural Language Processing:</strong> Examples of such applications are machine translation, text generation and speech recognition.</li><li><strong>Time Series Forecasting:</strong> Projection of values in the future starting point from values found in the past.</li><li><strong>Sequential Data Analysis:</strong> Forecasting and synthesizing patterns in a number of contexts.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Applications of DNN, CNN, and LSTM</h2><p>All of these neural network architectures, DNN, CNN, and LSTM, have been used widely in different fields because of their strengths. Some of the particular uses of each of the types of network are explained as follows:</p><h3 class="h3">Applications of DNN</h3><p><strong>1. Healthcare</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Disease Prediction:</strong> DNNs are employed in risk assessment of diseases, including diabetes, cancer and heart disease, based on patient information.</li><li><strong>Drug Discovery:</strong> In drug discovery, the architectures are used to determine the effectiveness of certain compounds by analyzing their structures.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Finance</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Credit Scoring:</strong> Currently, financial institutions use DNNs to evaluate individuals' credit histories and financial status.</li><li><strong>Algorithmic Trading:</strong> The DNNs are used to predict the trends in past market data, which can then be used in trading activities.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Natural Language Processing (NLP)</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Text Classification:</strong> Due to their non-linear learning nature on complex datasets, DNNs have been applied to several problems, including, but not limited to spam detection, sentiment analysis and topic classification.</li><li><strong>Language Modeling:</strong> In language modeling, DNNs estimate the probability of the next computerized word in a sequence, which is the basic structure of many applications, including autocomplete or text generation.</li></ul><p><strong>4. Manufacturing</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Quality Control:</strong> DNNs are employed in analysing data from sensors and image data to identify defects in products during the manufacturing process. Predictive Maintenance: The use of DNNs makes it possible to analyze the provided machinery data to predict the likelihood of likely failures, suggesting maintenance works in advance.</li></ul><p><strong>5. E-commerce</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Recommendation Systems:</strong> DNNs serve recommendation applications that help companies recommend their products to the client based on their past buying preferences.</li><li><strong>Customer Segmentation:</strong> The Customer's data is analyzed by DNNs to segment customers for marketing processes and strategies.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Applications of Convolutional Neural Networks</h3><p><strong>1. Computer Vision</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Image Classification:</strong> The CNNs are indispensable elements of image classification tasks, such as detecting objects in an image.</li><li><strong>Object Detection:</strong> The CNNs are used for object detection and segmentation in images, which is most important in self-driving cars, security systems, etc.</li><li><strong>Image Segmentation:</strong> The CNNs are used for object detection, predicting an object's location in a given image. In image segmentation, CNNs classify each pixel of an image, allowing real-life applications such as medical imaging and scene understanding.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Healthcare</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Medical Imaging:</strong> The CNNs are widely applied to detect diseases, such as tumors, fractures, and others, through medical images, including X-rays, MRIs, and CT scans.</li><li><strong>Dermatology:</strong> The CNNs contribute to the diagnosis of skin diseases by categorizing images of skin lesions and moles.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Autonomous Vehicles</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Lane Detection:</strong> Another use of the CNNs is to recognize lane markers on the roads, which is essential for level 5 autonomy.</li><li><strong>Object Recognition:</strong> The CNNs is to assist self-driving cars in identifying objects such as people, traffic signs and other cars.</li></ul><p><strong>4. Augmentation and Virtual Reality</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Scene Understanding:</strong> They are employed in AR and VR applications to enable the interpretation and comprehension of 3D scenes, making user's interaction with a virtual environment possible.</li><li><strong>Gesture Recognition:</strong> In AR/VR systems, CNNs can detect hand and body gestures, thus enabling the identification of control methods.</li></ul><p><strong>5. Robotics</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Visual Perception:</strong> CNNs help robots develop vision capability by using visual sensors when mapping, manipulating, and interacting with people.</li><li><strong>Quality Inspection:</strong> CNNs are used in robot manufacturing industries to recognize the defects of manufactured products.</li></ul><p><strong>6. Art and Design</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Style Transfer:</strong> CNNs are images that depict artwork to apply one image to another, especially to artwork, impressions and other artistic features in photos and videos.</li><li><strong>Image Enhancement:</strong> They are used in applications such as super-resolution in which low-resolution images are reconstructed to high resolution.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Applications of Long-Short Term Memory Networks (LSTMs)</h3><p><strong>1. Natural Language Processing (NLP)</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Machine Translation:</strong> LSTMs are used to drive machine translation, a large category that deals with the translation of text and speech in real-time.</li><li><strong>Text Generation:</strong> They can even write texts or create texts that look similar to the input data, which is used in the creation of chatbots, content and even stories.</li><li><strong>Speech Recognition:</strong> LSTM networks are useful in speech-to-text applications, where they analyze and transcribe spoken language.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Time Series Forecasting</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Stock Price Prediction:</strong> These are used to predict the future stock price based on past data with the help of investing or trading plans.</li><li><strong>Weather Forecasting:</strong> This is due to the ability of LSTMs to pinpoint accurate weather forecasts through pattern recognition of historical weather data.</li><li><strong>Energy Load Forecasting:</strong> LSTMs also help utility companies anticipate their consumers' future energy demands, which helps them plan for the future.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Healthcare</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Predictive Analytics:</strong> LSTMs are used to make a prognosis for a patient, with output in the form of the patient's future health state, such as readmission, disease rate, and other related aspects.</li><li><strong>Wearable Data Analysis:</strong> LSTMs for continuous data from wearable devices, including HR or step count, to identify abnormalities and suggest improvements in health.</li></ul><p><strong>4. Finance</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Anomaly Detection in Transactions:</strong> LSTMs are used to detect fraud because they analyze a series of transactions and identify the paths that are most likely to be fraudulent.</li><li><strong>Customer Behaviour Analysis:</strong> LSTMs provide the financial industry with a tool to understand customers' needs over time and tailor financial products and services.</li></ul><p><strong>5. Speech Processing</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Speech Synthesis:</strong> LSTMs synthesize human-like speech by learning acoustic patterns in the data used in modern voice-controlled devices and TTS systems.</li><li><strong>Voice Biometric Recognition:</strong> The LSTMs, which deliver some of the security attributes for devices and applications related to voice, can detect people.</li></ul><p><strong>6. Robotics</strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>Control Systems:</strong> LSTMs are applied to control robot systems, especially in activities that involve sequential decisions and learning from experience.</li><li><strong>Motion Prediction:</strong> LSTMs also assist in predicting the future actions of the robots or people to improve interaction in common operating space.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Implementation</h3><p><strong>Example: Time Series Prediction</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># Load the TensorBoard notebook extension.
%load_ext tensorboard

# import tensorflow and tensorboard
import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow import keras
import tensorboard

# Helper libraries
import random
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import datetime
</textarea></div><p><strong>Explanation</strong></p><ul class="points"><li>It loads the TensorBoard extension to visualize training right into a Jupyter notebook.</li><li>It brings into the project the core modules of TensorFlow, a library designed to facilitate deep learning, and Keras, a high-level neural networks API.</li><li>Use the TensorBoard to log the training reports and metrics.</li><li>Import the 'random' module to generate random numbers.</li><li>Imports NumPy to carry out numerical computations of extensive use of arrays and matrices.</li><li>It uses Matplotlib, which is a 'pyplot' library for plots and data visualization.</li><li>The datetime module is used to create the timestamps, which are used for the date and time.</li><li>The setup lays down the conditions for constructing and training models in TensorFlow/Keras with logging functionalities in TensorBoard.</li></ul><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">Num_train_data = 4000
Num_test_data=1000
Timestep=0.1
Tm=np.arange(0,(num_train_data+num_test_data)*timestep,timestep);
Y=np.sin™+np.sin(tm*np.pi/2)+np.sin(tm*(-3*np.pi/2)) 

SNR = 10
Ypn = y + np.random.normal(0,10**(-SNR/20),len(y))
Plt.plot(ypn,'*')
Plt.show()
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><img alt="Comparison of DNN, CNN, and LSTM Using TensorFlow/Keras" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/machine-learning/images/a-comparison-of-dnn-cnn-and-lstm-using-tensorflow-keras.png"/><p><strong>Explanation</strong></p><ul class="points"><li>Define 'num_train_data' and 'num_test_data' as 4000 and 1000, respectively.</li><li>The second parameter to call is setting the timestep to '0. 1'.</li><li>Create the time vector tm through 'np. arange from 0 to (num_train_data + num_test_data) * timestep 'with the change in t 0 timestep.</li><li>Create the composite sinusoidal signal y by summing three sine waves: Namely, np, sin(tm), np, sin(tm*np. pi/2), and np. sin(tm*(-3*np. pi/2)).</li><li>The step-by-step instructions include: 'Position the transducer at the region of interest and ensure the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is at 10 dB.'</li><li>Applying noise Gaussian to the signal y with the help of np in order to obtain ypn. random. normal where noise level is defined as '10**(-SNR/20)'.</li><li>In this case, the noisy signal ypn must be plotted as a scatter plot using the high-level function 'plt.'</li><li>plot' with asterisks ('*)' used as markers.</li><li>Perform the plot with the help of 'plt. show()'.</li></ul><p><strong>Prediction Using DNN</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># Let us look at CNN next
cnn_numinputs = 16 
num_cnn_features = 1
train_data = []
cnn_train_batch = 0

# prepare training data
for k in range(num_train_data-cnn_numinputs-1):
  train_data = np.concatenate((train_data,ypn[k:k+cnn_numinputs]));
  cnn_train_batch = cnn_train_batch + 1  
train_data = np.reshape(train_data, (cnn_train_batch,cnn_numinputs,num_cnn_features))
train_labels = y[cnn_numinputs:cnn_train_batch+cnn_numinputs]

# define the model
def cnn_keras_model():
  # Use 4 tap conv. filter and process each set of input samples 3 times (i.e., 3 different filters)
  # This means each input sample will create 3 such sample 
  # Max pooling then picks 4 out of this 12 resulting in 3 samples for each input sample
  # this goes to a Dense layer that provides 16 outputs, 
  # followed by another dense layer that provides 1 output 
  model = keras.Sequential()
  model.add(tf.keras.layers.Conv1D(filters=3, kernel_size=4, activation='tanh', 
                    input_shape=(train_data.shape[1],train_data.shape[2])))
  model.add(tf.keras.layers.AveragePooling1D(pool_size=4))
  model.add(tf.keras.layers.Flatten())
  model.add(tf.keras.layers.Dense(16, activation='relu'))
  model.add(tf.keras.layers.Dense(1))
  model.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='mse')
  model.summary()
  return model

# train the model
EPOCHS=100
model=cnn_keras_model()
strt_time=datetime.datetime.now()
history=model.fit(train_data,train_labels,epochs=EPOCHS,
                  validation_split=0.2,verbose=0,
                  callbacks=[])
curr_time=datetime.datetime.now()
timedelta=curr_time - strt_time
cnn_train_time=timedelta.total_seconds()
print("CNN training done.Time elapsed:",timedelta.total_seconds(),"s")                  
#plt.plot(history.epoch,np.array(history.history['val_loss']),label='Val loss')
#plt.show()
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output</strong></p><p><strong>Model: "sequential"</strong></p><table class="alt"><tbody><tr><th>Layer (type)</th><th>Output Shape</th><th>Param #</th></tr><tr><td>conv1d (Conv1D)</td><td>(None, 13, 3)</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>average_pooling1d(AveragePooling1D)</td><td>(None, 3, 3)</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Flatten (Flatten)</td><td>(None, 9)</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>dense (Dense)</td><td>(None, 16)</td><td>160</td></tr><tr><td>dense_1 (Dense)</td><td>(None, 1)</td><td>17</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Total params:</strong> 192 (768.00 B)</p><p><strong>Trainable params:</strong> 192 (768.00 B)</p><p><strong>Non-trainable params:</strong> 0 (0.00 B)</p><p>CNN training done.Time elapsed: 33.45632 s</p><p><strong>Explanation</strong></p><ul class="points"><li>The parameters are to be set as follows: set 'cnn_numinputs to 16', which determines the number of CNN input samples.</li><li>Dominant set: set 'num_cnn_features to 1, about the number of features per input sample was set.</li><li>Start with the 'train_data' list being empty so that it helps in storing the training data.</li><li>Start counting the number for training batches with 'cnn_train_batch' equal to 0 at the beginning.</li><li>To create the training data, the range should go from '0 to num_train_data-cnn_numinputs - 1'.</li><li>For each iteration, we append a segment of the 'ypn (from index k to k + cnn_numinputs) to the train_data'.</li><li>It is necessary to increase the value of 'cnn_train_batch' by 1 with each iteration.</li><li>Finally, to recover train_data to have the dimensions of 'cnn_train_batch, cnn_numinputs, num_cnn_features'.</li><li>Create the new series 'train_labels' from the corresponding y values from 'cnn_numinputs' up to 'cnn_train_batch + cnn_numinputs'.</li><li>Having understood the elements of a CNN model, we proceeded to set it up using a sequential Keras model.</li><li>Now, it is time to add one more layer - the convolutional layer of the 1D type; it has 3 filters; the size of the kernel is 4; the activation function is a tanh. The input shape is set to be the second and third dimensions of train_data, that is, train_data. shape[1:].</li><li>Include an average pooling layer with the option to enter the pool size 4.</li><li>Reshape the output of the pooling layer to be a two-dimensional array.</li><li>Follow it with a dense layer with 16 units and relu as its activation function.</li><li>A dense layer in the end layer should have one neuron only.</li><li>Finish the model with Adam optimizer and mse Loss function.</li><li>Print the model summary.</li><li>Summarize Set 'EPOCHS to 100' for training.</li><li>You may call the 'cnn_keras_model()' function to start the model.</li><li>Save the current time at which the start button has been pressed as 'strt_time'.</li><li>Fit the model to 'train_data' with 'train_labels' and cross-validate with 20% data over the given number of epochs.</li><li>Store the end time as 'curr_time' and then sum the overall CNN training time in seconds, 'cnn_train_time'.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Advantages of LSTM</h3><li><strong>Captures Temporal Dependencies:</strong> LSTM models are specifically suited for dealing with sequential data. Their architecture normally enables them to capture temporal dependencies over time. This is particularly relevant in time series analysis, natural language processing, and all use cases where sequence is important.</li><li><strong>Avoids the Vanishing Gradient Problem:</strong> The major drawback of standard RNNs has been the vanishing gradient, which limits the trainability of models on sequential data. LSTMs tackle the vanishing gradient problem through the use of specific gating mechanisms, namely the input, output and forget gates.</li><li><strong>Handles Long Sequences:</strong> LSTMs, due to their architecture, can learn and make predictions on long sequences of data better than regular RNNs. This is especially important in operations such as speech recognition, language translation, and video analysis.</li><li><strong>Memory Efficiency:</strong> LSTMs' forget-and-remember mechanism is suitable for dealing with the massive temporal data stream coming with input, as it suppresses or enhances particular portions depending on what is required.</li><li><strong>Flexibility in Model Design:</strong> LTSMs are flexible when integrated with other neural network frameworks, such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), to form Advanced Neural Network Architectures that incorporate both temporal and spatial feature learning. This benefit is especially evident in tasks such as video classification, in which temporal and spatial features are significant.</li><li><strong>Resilient to Noise:</strong> The experimental results show that noise does not effectively respond to LSTM networks compared with simple models because the change in the environment is rather reactive, and it looks for important temporal patterns to ignore noise.</li><p></p><h3 class="h3">Advantages of DNN</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Versatility:</strong> DNNs are very flexible, showing great results in various problem areas such as image recognition, speech recognition, analysis of the time series, and others, including even game AI. This enables them to model dependency in large data sets that may not be linear in nature.</li><li><strong>Feature Extraction:</strong> DNNs can learn features from raw data, hence requiring less feature engineering than traditional deep learning models. These abilities make them suitable for tasks where features in data are organized, such as recognizing objects in an image, where quite different features are discerned at the different abstraction levels.</li><li><strong>High Accuracy:</strong> As will be seen in the later sections, DNNs, when well-trained on large datasets, can reach impressive performance on different tasks. Their depth makes them good at identifying interrelations in data patterns, hence yielding high accuracy in forecasting.</li><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> DNNs can work with big data and complex problems, thus making them possible to scale up. ANNs and NNs can be expanded by increasing the number of layers or neurons when there is adequate computational capacity and massive data to feed the networks, which makes them applicable to solving greater problems.</li><li><strong>Transfer Learning:</strong> DNNs can use transfer learning, which entails using a large database-trained model and fine-tuning it for use in a small database. This is particularly useful where there is limited labeled data, as it helps models enjoy good performance with little data and time.</li><li><strong>Parallelism and Efficiency:</strong> Modern DNN architectures can be easily trained in parallelized form on GPUs, making dealing with large datasets possible. The use of hardware accelerators and optimized libraries also speeds up the training of deep networks.</li><li><strong>Customization:</strong> The DNNs have the advantage that their architecture is fully configurable by practitioners, for example, the number of layers, the type of layers, or activation functions. This has made the development of quite specific models for specific uses possible.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Applications of CNN</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong>Automatic Feature Extraction:</strong> This study identifies Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) because they are capable of learning and extracting relevant features from the raw input data autonomously and specifically in images. This eliminates the need to perform feature extraction individually since the network is capable of identifying edges, textures, shapes, creases and other intricate features inherent in data automatically.</li><li><strong>Spatial Hierarchy:</strong> CNNs have the capacity to capture spatial hierarchies in the data through the layers of convection and pooling. Earlier layers are usually tasked with detecting simple elements of an image, including the edges, while later layers identify higher-level objects, such as shapes and objects. The hierarchical feature learning proposed in this work is important in tasks such as image and video analysis.</li><li><strong>Parameter Sharing:</strong> Indeed, in CNNs, the same weights (filters) are thrice used for all the areas of the input. This parameter sharing makes CNNs bear far fewer parameters than fully connected networks; it also minimizes the chances of overfitting.</li><li><strong>Translation Invariance:</strong> A CNN, in particular, is naturally translation invariant, so it can identify an object in an image irrespective of its orientation. This property is achieved through convolutional layers, which put filters on the input and then shift them to scan for any pattern present in the image.</li><li><strong>Efficient Computation:</strong> CNNs possess local connections and shared weights that make the computations less than those required in fully connected networks of similar size. This makes CNNs flexible enough to scale to large images and datasets, and thus, they can be employed for real-time applications.</li><li><strong>Applicability to Various Data Types:</strong> Although CNNs offer optimal solutions for image data, they can also be applied to other types of data, such as audio, time series, or textual data, provided such data can be represented as one-, two-, or three-dimensional grids. This flexibility enables CNNs to be applied in areas other than computer vision, as mentioned below.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>DNNs, CNNs and LSTMs, for instance, are all neural networks that possess different structures that make them more applicable to certain types of data and tasks. DNNs are widely used and applicable to most real-life applications. CNNs are designed to work best on images and feature extraction, and LSTMs work best on sequential data due to their capability of capturing long-term dependencies.</p><p>TensorFlow/Keras offers features for implementing such architectures, making it easier for researchers and practitioners to develop, compute, and deploy the models. It is, therefore, important to learn about the properties and applicability of different types of networks so as to identify the most suitable for the problem at hand.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="bayes-theorem-in-machine-learning">Bayes Theorem in Machine learning</a></span></div><br/><br/><div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="epoch" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="bayes-theorem-in-machine-learning" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/><br/></td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="stationarity-tests-in-time-series"><h3 class="h3">Stationarity Tests in Time Series</h3><p class="related-post-content">Time series data is widely used in many disciplines, including signal processing, meteorology, finance, and economics. It is composed of observations that are gathered successively over an extended period of time. Stationarity is a key idea in the analysis of time series data. Stable statistical characteristics,...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 6 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-3.4-1.3-6.6-3.7-9.1l-82.4-83c-4.9-5.2-13.1-5.3-18.2-.3z"></path></svg></span></p></a></li><li><a class="related-post-item" href="introduction-to-causality-in-machine-learni